The map $\mathbf x \mapsto A\mathbf x$ satisfies $A(\mathbf x + \mathbf y) = A\mathbf x + A\mathbf y$ and $A(c\mathbf x) = cA\mathbf x$. In the plane write $\mathbf e_1 = (1, 0)$ and $\mathbf e_2 = (0, 1)$. Then $A\mathbf e_1$ and $A\mathbf e_2$ are the two columns of $A$, and since $(x, y) = x\mathbf e_1 + y\mathbf e_2$, the columns determine where every point goes. To write down the matrix of a transformation, record the images of $\mathbf e_1$ and $\mathbf e_2$ as its columns. Some examples (compare [[vectors-plane]] and [[trig]]):
- rotation by the angle $\theta$ about the origin sends $\mathbf e_1 \mapsto (\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$ and $\mathbf e_2 \mapsto (-\sin\theta, \cos\theta)$, so it is $R_\theta = \begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\ \sin\theta & \cos\theta \end{pmatrix}$;
- reflection in the $x$-axis is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix}$;
- the shear $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ sends $(x, y) \mapsto (x + y, y)$, sliding each horizontal line sideways by its height;
- the projection $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ sends $(x, y) \mapsto (x, 0)$, flattening the plane onto the $x$-axis.

The unit square with sides $\mathbf e_1, \mathbf e_2$ is carried to the parallelogram with sides the two columns, which may be flattened to a segment or a point.
